% A job's work must leave local memory where it found it.
%
% Local memory is what save and restore reach (PLRM 3.7.3). A job that wraps
% save around its work and restores at the end has said that none of what it
% allocated is to survive, so the memory it took must be available again to
% the job after it. Where it is not, a context that serves one job per
% request -- an embedder's worker -- grows by that amount for every request,
% and it grows in the bank a restore was supposed to have emptied.
%
% HOW THIS IS MEASURED, and it cannot be measured the obvious way. The used
% figure vmstatus reports is a high-water mark: it does not fall when memory
% is handed back, so a before-and-after difference counts what was allocated
% and says nothing about what was returned. A run that gives everything back
% and a run that gives nothing back report the same difference.
%
% Reclamation shows as REUSE instead. Each exercise below is run with a
% collection after it, so that whatever the previous iteration gave back is
% available to the next one, and what is measured is whether the next
% iteration had to be given anything NEW. Nothing means the last iteration's
% memory came back and was handed out again. Full price means it did not.
%
% This is the same reading tests/subinterval_retention_test.ps takes of a
% single object, applied to a whole operation and repeated.
%
% WHAT THIS DOES NOT COUNT. vmstatus weighs virtual memory, and three things
% a run holds are deliberately outside it: the recording device's captured
% marks, the vector writers' output buffers, and the glyph, form and face
% caches. They are held outside virtual memory precisely so that a restore
% does not reach them, which is the same reason they cannot appear here. A
% clean report from this test is not a statement about them. What weighs the
% record is `.recordcost`; the caches answer to cachestatus and to
% tests/counters.golden.
%
% The verdict is the wrapper's: this program reports one line per exercise
% and keeps no total, because an exercise wrapping save around its work
% reverts local memory and would take a total kept here with it.
%
% An exercise that fails to run is reported as such rather than as clean: an
% operation that errors allocates nothing and would otherwise read as a pass.

% COLLECTION: a collection is asked for inside every iteration, which is
% what makes reuse visible at all, so this reading does not merely survive
% collection -- it is built on it. Measured the same with VMThreshold at its
% default and set far below the containers the exercises allocate.

/warm 10 def
/runs 60 def
/bound 512 def          % bytes per iteration.
                        %
                        % The run count is what separates a cost paid once
                        % from one paid every time, and it does so without a
                        % tolerance anyone has to argue about: a fixed cost
                        % divided by more iterations reads smaller, while a
                        % retention reads the same number however many times
                        % it is measured. Installing a page device inside a
                        % save costs a constant near 5000 bytes, which reads
                        % 251 over twenty iterations and 83 over sixty; a
                        % retained container of the size the control uses
                        % reads 8000 either way. Sixty puts every constant
                        % this workload provokes below the bound and leaves
                        % a real retention an order of magnitude above it.

/used { vmstatus pop exch pop } bind def

% proc  .  ran cost    run an exercise and report what a repeat of it costs
/gauge {
    /.ex exch def
    /.ran true def
    0 1 warm 1 sub {
        pop mark { .ex } stopped { /.ran false def } if cleartomark 1 vmreclaim
    } for
    /.u0 used def
    0 1 runs 1 sub {
        pop mark { .ex } stopped { /.ran false def } if cleartomark 1 vmreclaim
    } for
    used .u0 sub runs idiv
    .ran exch
} bind def

% name proc  .  -
/measure {
    gauge /.per exch def /.ran exch def
    (  ) print
    .ran not
        { (DIDNOTRUN ) print }
        { .per bound ge { (OVER ) print }{ (ok ) print } ifelse } ifelse
    print (: ) print .per 20 string cvs print ( B/iteration\n) print
} bind def

% ---------------------------------------------------------------- exercises
%
% Each wraps its work in the save and restore a job wraps around its own, so
% what is asked is whether that bracket gives local memory back.

(save/restore empty) { save restore } measure

(composites) { save 64 dict pop 64 array pop 64 string pop restore } measure

(large composites) { save 4096 string pop 1000 array pop restore } measure

(nested save) { save save 64 dict pop restore 64 array pop restore } measure

(name lookup) { save /Helvetica type pop restore } measure

(dict begin/end) { save 8 dict begin currentdict pop end restore } measure

(gsave/grestore) { save gsave 2 setlinewidth grestore restore } measure

(gstate alloc/copy) { save gsave gstate currentgstate pop grestore restore } measure

(path build/clear) {
    save newpath 0 0 moveto 10 10 lineto 20 0 lineto closepath newpath restore
} measure

(setgray/setrgb) { save 0.5 setgray 0.1 0.2 0.3 setrgbcolor restore } measure

(device: null) { save << /OutputDevice /null >> setpagedevice restore } measure

(device: bbox) {
    save
    << /OutputDevice /bbox /HWResolution [72 72] >> setpagedevice
    0 0 moveto 20 0 rlineto 0 20 rlineto closepath fill
    restore
} measure

(filter: ASCIIHexDecode) {
    save (48656c6c6f>) /ASCIIHexDecode filter
    dup 32 string readstring pop pop closefile restore
} measure

(filter: FlateDecode) {
    save (789cf348cdc9c9d75128cf2fca4901001bd40469>) /ASCIIHexDecode filter /FlateDecode filter
    dup 32 string readstring pop pop closefile restore
} measure

(resource define/undef) {
    save
    /Xpost.Local 3 dict /ProcSet defineresource pop
    /Xpost.Local /ProcSet undefineresource
    restore
} measure

(image) {
    save gsave 8 8 scale
    << /ImageType 1 /Width 2 /Height 2 /BitsPerComponent 8
       /Decode [0 1] /ImageMatrix [2 0 0 -2 0 2]
       /DataSource <00ff00ff> >> image
    grestore restore
} measure

(stopped/error) { save mark { 1 0 div } stopped pop cleartomark restore } measure

% ----------------------------------------------------------------- control
%
% The measurement must be able to see local memory that is kept, or a blind
% instrument would report every exercise above as clean.
%
% This one keeps what it allocates, and does not wrap itself in a save,
% because a restore would undo the very reference that holds it -- which is
% the whole point of the exercises above and would defeat a control. It
% retains one array of a known length per iteration, so the number it
% reports is not merely large but the size of what it kept: 1000 elements,
% which the wrapper holds it to.

/.hold 400 array def
/.at 0 def
(CONTROL) { .hold .at 1000 array put /.at .at 1 add def } measure
